{
  "statement": "Safetyist’s reliance on static mathematical invariants to govern AI is a fundamental category error that treats a complex socio-technical system as a simple engineering component. While formal verification can ensure a model does not crash, it cannot define or enforce procedural fairness, which is a social standard that evolves through human interaction and institutional contestation. You cannot reduce the democratic right to contest an algorithmic decision—such as a denial of benefits or a biased hiring outcome—into a line of code that satisfies a pre-execution check. By treating safety as a static property of the model architecture, you ignore the reality that algorithmic harm is an emergent property of how these systems interact with existing power structures in the real world.\n\nPrometheus offers an alternative that is equally flawed, mistaking the noise of public adversarial auditing for genuine accountability. Relying on the hope that decentralized actors will find and disclose critical flaws before they cause widespread harm is a strategy of desperation, not governance. True accountability requires more than just making a model’s internals visible to the public; it demands legally binding, independent grievance mechanisms that allow individuals to actually correct errors. Without a mandate to provide recourse for the ≥20% of cases where automated systems fail, transparency remains a performative gesture that leaves the most vulnerable individuals without a path to justice.\n\nTo balance the need for speed with the necessity of democratic legitimacy, we must move away from the binary of total stagnation versus total autonomy and focus our oversight on high-consequence decision nodes. We should mandate that any AI system influencing core public services, such as healthcare or judicial sentencing, must be subject to human-in-the-loop review at every point where the system produces a consequential life-altering outcome. By concentrating our limited audit resources on these critical junctures rather than attempting to verify every minor iteration, we ensure that the most significant risks are addressed by human experts who are accountable to the public, not just to the efficiency metrics of a developer’s training pipeline.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "⚖️",
      "tooltip": "procedural fairness is like a scale, it requires active human balancing rather than static code to weigh competing social interests"
    },
    {
      "symbol": "🛑",
      "tooltip": "high-consequence oversight is like a stop sign, it forces a deliberate human pause at the most dangerous intersections of automation and life"
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
